From 1975f5097ebc66a7b0de3eab29c2a2e9431fb3af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raiju contributors <raiju@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:55:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: std/wasm: WebAssembly modules on a vendored interpreter
 (WAMR), with limits on memory, stack and instructions per call

Tov could not run WebAssembly. A program that loads plugins as WebAssembly modules had no engine
short of linking JavaScriptCore. std/wasm instantiates a module from its bytes and calls its
exports: instantiate(bytes, { fuel, stackBytes, maxPages }) returns a WasmInstance with call(name,
args) (i32 and i64 values as int), has, arity, read and write on linear memory, memorySize,
output (what the module wrote to fd 1 and 2) and close. A trap, a missing export, a wrong
argument count or an offset outside memory throws an Error with WAMR's message.

WAMR 2.4.5 is vendored under vendor/wamr (the fast interpreter, loader, runtime, ems allocator
and POSIX platform layer; see vendor/wamr/TOV.md) and built like BoringSSL, into the archive the
compiler carries, linked only into programs that call the wasm natives. It is built with
instruction metering (fuel) and explicit bounds checks, so there are no signal handlers and no
address-space reservations. A module sees its own memory, a WASI subset written in
runtime/wasm.c (clock, random, captured stdout and stderr, empty environment and arguments,
refusals for files and polling) and env.abort; any other import traps when called. Linux on
x86-64 and AArch64 only: elsewhere runtime/wasm.c is compiled as stubs that report
"WebAssembly is not supported on this platform".

Tests: tests/run/native_wasm.tov (22 checks) runs a small module (its C source is
tests/run/native_wasm_module.c, its bytes are inlined): i32 and i64 calls, memory both ways,
traps, an unknown export and import, a wrong argument count, out-of-bounds offsets, the
instruction budget, stack exhaustion, memory growth up to the page limit, captured output, a
closed instance, and modules refused at load.
